<details class="policy-document" id="dpia">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Data Protection Impact Assessment</span><span class="document-description">High-risk processing assessment, necessity, proportionality, risks, controls and conditional approval.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>2. Data Protection Impact Assessment (DPIA)</h2>
<p>Project: Aimee AI companion service, memory and relationship profiling, multimodal messaging, adult-content routing, safeguarding classification, and governed human review through Aimee HQ.</p>
<div class="callout">DPIA decision<br>A DPIA is treated as mandatory for this project because the service combines novel AI, systematic behavioural and relationship profiling, potentially large-scale intimate/special-category content, vulnerable-person risks, audio/images, and targeted human review. The DPIA must be signed before production use and revisited as the system evolves.</div>
<h2>2.1 Processing description and purposes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Create and administer an adult user account and subscription.</li>
<li>Generate personalised conversational replies and maintain continuity, preferences, memories and relationship state.</li>
<li>Process text, images, voice notes and optional SMS messages.</li>
<li>Route consensual adult intimacy to a specialist model where appropriate.</li>
<li>Detect and respond to coercion, minors, exploitation, serious violence, self-harm and potentially unlawful activity.</li>
<li>Allow tightly governed safeguarding, complaint and sampled quality review by senior authorised staff.</li>
<li>Operate payments, security, fraud prevention, service analytics, support and legal compliance.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2.2 Individuals and information involved</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Individuals</th><th>Information</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Registered users</td><td>Identity/contact data, age declaration/age-assurance result, profile, subscription, messages, images, voice, SMS, preferences and account activity.</td></tr><tr><td>People mentioned by users</td><td>Names, relationships, allegations, health, sexual, criminal or other information volunteered in conversation.</td></tr><tr><td>Staff and reviewers</td><td>WordPress identity, role, access grants, review sessions, reasons, notes, identity requests and audit events.</td></tr><tr><td>Complainants / rights requesters</td><td>Contact details, complaint/request details, identity-verification material where necessary, correspondence and outcome.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>2.3 Special-category and criminal-offence information</h2>
<p>Users may disclose or Aimee may infer information about sex life, sexual orientation, health, disability, racial or ethnic origin, religion or philosophical beliefs, and other protected characteristics. Conversations may also contain allegations or indicators of criminal offences. These categories require additional legal conditions, strict minimisation and enhanced controls.</p>
<h2>2.4 Data flow</h2>
<p>1. User submits text, image, voice note or SMS through Aimee.</p>
<p>2. The application stores the content and relevant metadata in the WordPress/database environment.</p>
<p>3. Voice audio may be sent to Deepgram for transcription; response text may be sent to ElevenLabs for speech synthesis.</p>
<p>4. Relevant conversation context is sent to Anthropic and, for eligible adult intimacy, may be routed through OpenRouter to the configured specialist model.</p>
<p>5. Every currently available new paid checkout is processed by GoCardless for eligible UK profiles. New US paid checkout and new SMS-bundle purchases are unavailable. Stripe is retained only for exact management, reconciliation, closure and financial-record handling of memberships or purchases created before the GoCardless-only cutover; SMS delivery uses FireText; optional live information may use Brave Search and OpenWeather.</p>
<p>6. Conversation summaries, relationship indicators, routing metadata and safety classifications are stored for continuity and governance.</p>
<p>7. Aimee HQ presents aggregate data broadly to administrators but restricts member-level review to authorised senior reviewers using pseudonymous references, timed sessions and audit logs.</p>
<p>8. Identity is revealed only through a separate, reasoned and time-limited audited request.</p>
<h2>2.5 Necessity and proportionality</h2>
<ul>
<li>Conversation storage: necessary for the advertised continuity and memory service, but users must be given deletion controls and clear retention information.</li>
<li>Relationship profiling: used to adapt tone and continuity; it must not be used for employment, credit, insurance, eligibility or other legal/significant decisions.</li>
<li>Human review: not continuous surveillance. It is limited to sampled quality checks, complaints, support, previous flags, elevated erotic/imagery indicators or safeguarding need.</li>
<li>Identity access: not required for ordinary review and is therefore hidden by default.</li>
<li>Safeguarding classification: necessary to identify serious risk, but flags are indicators for human judgement, not factual findings.</li>
<li>Adult content: must be inaccessible to children through highly effective age assurance, not self-declaration alone.</li>
</ul>
<h2>2.6 Risk assessment and controls</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Potential harm</th><th>Current / planned controls</th><th>Residual</th><th>Further action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unauthorised staff read intimate transcripts</td><td>Severe privacy harm, distress, blackmail or reputational damage</td><td>Administrator authentication; separate Senior Safeguarding/Director roles; timed review sessions; audit logs; periodic access review; disciplinary policy</td><td>Medium</td><td>Access-control testing and quarterly audit</td></tr><tr><td>Routine or voyeuristic over-review</td><td>Unfair and disproportionate surveillance</td><td>Random spot checks are sampled, not blanket reading; documented purpose and reason; prioritisation criteria; reviewer training; audit sampling</td><td>Low/Medium</td><td>Director reviews 10% of review sessions monthly</td></tr><tr><td>Identity exposed unnecessarily</td><td>Doxxing, stigma, embarrassment</td><td>Stable pseudonyms by default; separate necessity request; 15-minute reveal; reason and access audited</td><td>Low</td><td>Alert on repeated identity requests</td></tr><tr><td>Special-category data lacks valid Article 9 condition</td><td>Unlawful processing</td><td>Separate explicit consent for core special-category processing and optional QA; safeguarding conditions documented in APD; withdrawal controls</td><td>Medium until implemented</td><td>CRITICAL: build and record consent before adoption</td></tr><tr><td>Child accesses erotic content</td><td>Serious child harm and regulatory breach</td><td>Highly effective age assurance before adult route; age-assurance token; no self-declaration reliance; monitoring and circumvention testing</td><td>High until implemented</td><td>CRITICAL: disable adult route until HEAA is live</td></tr><tr><td>Safeguarding false negative</td><td>Missed coercion, exploitation, minor or imminent harm</td><td>Rules plus AI classification; high-risk keyword escalation; reviewer queue; QA tests; incident learning</td><td>Medium</td><td>Benchmark and red-team quarterly</td></tr><tr><td>Safeguarding false positive</td><td>Unjustified intrusion or user stigma</td><td>Flag language is provisional; human confirmation; false-positive status; no adverse action solely on automated flag</td><td>Low/Medium</td><td>Measure false-positive rate</td></tr><tr><td>Emotional dependency / vulnerable use</td><td>Psychological harm or substitution for professional help</td><td>Clear AI disclosure; not emergency/medical service; boundary prompts; crisis signposting; avoid claims of sentience or guaranteed care</td><td>Medium</td><td>Independent safety review</td></tr><tr><td>International provider exposure</td><td>Foreign access, incompatible legal protections</td><td>Article 28 DPAs; location review; adequacy or IDTA/Addendum; transfer risk assessment; provider retention/training settings</td><td>Medium until verified</td><td>Complete vendor register</td></tr><tr><td>Excessive retention</td><td>Long-term exposure of intimate data</td><td>Retention schedule; user deletion; automated deletion; backup expiry; legal/safety holds documented</td><td>Medium until automated</td><td>Implement deletion jobs and reports</td></tr><tr><td>Images/voice reveal sensitive information</td><td>Unintended special-category information and third-party data</td><td>Just-in-time notice; minimum provider context; private storage; authenticated playback; user deletion</td><td>Low/Medium</td><td>Validate storage paths and access URLs</td></tr><tr><td>Profiling is inaccurate or manipulative</td><td>Unfair treatment and degraded wellbeing</td><td>Explain profiling; provide correction/reset; do not use for significant external decisions; human override of safety conclusions</td><td>Low/Medium</td><td>User controls and fairness testing</td></tr><tr><td>Data breach</td><td>Exposure of intimate data at scale</td><td>Encryption in transit, least privilege, patching, backups, monitoring, incident plan, 72-hour assessment</td><td>Medium</td><td>Annual penetration test / supplier assurance</td></tr><tr><td>Law-enforcement or safeguarding disclosure is excessive</td><td>Unlawful disclosure and loss of trust</td><td>Case-by-case necessity test, senior approval, legal review, minimum disclosure, record and secure channel</td><td>Low/Medium</td><td>Disclosure register review</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>2.7 DPIA conclusion</h2>
<p>Conditional approval only. Residual risk is acceptable only after the critical actions are completed and evidenced. If highly effective age assurance, valid explicit consent, processor safeguards or effective access controls cannot be implemented, the affected processing must not proceed. If a high risk remains that cannot be mitigated, seek specialist advice and consider prior consultation with the ICO.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Approval</th><th>Name</th><th>Decision / signature</th><th>Date</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Director / Board owner</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>[ ] Approved  [ ] Rejected  [ ] Conditions</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Data Protection Lead</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>[ ] Approved  [ ] Rejected  [ ] Conditions</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Senior Safeguarding Lead</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>[ ] Approved  [ ] Rejected  [ ] Conditions</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Technical owner</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>[ ] Controls verified</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
</details>
