<details class="policy-document" id="lawful-basis">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Lawful bases and Legitimate Interests Assessments</span><span class="document-description">Article 6 bases, special-category conditions and balancing assessments.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>4. Lawful-basis record and Legitimate Interests Assessments</h2>
<h2>4.1 Lawful-basis map</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Article 6 basis</th><th>Additional condition / note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Provide account, conversation, memory, voice, imagery and subscription</td><td>Contract: Article 6(1)(b)</td><td>For special-category content deliberately shared, obtain explicit consent under Article 9(2)(a).</td></tr><tr><td>Payments, tax and statutory records</td><td>Contract and legal obligation: Articles 6(1)(b)/(c)</td><td>Financial records only; avoid copying chat content.</td></tr><tr><td>Account security, fraud prevention and abuse controls</td><td>Legitimate interests: Article 6(1)(f), or recognised legitimate interest where the statutory wording applies</td><td>Document necessity and balancing; special/criminal data requires separate condition.</td></tr><tr><td>Optional sampled relationship-quality review</td><td>Legitimate interests for ordinary data</td><td>Obtain separate optional explicit consent before reviewers access sensitive/special-category content for QA.</td></tr><tr><td>Safeguarding children or adults at risk</td><td>Legitimate interests and, in an emergency, vital interests</td><td>Article 9(2)(g) with DPA 2018 Schedule 1 paragraph 18; APD required. Article 9(2)(c) may apply in a genuine vital-interest emergency.</td></tr><tr><td>Preventing or detecting unlawful acts</td><td>Legitimate interests</td><td>Article 9(2)(g) and DPA 2018 Schedule 1 paragraph 10; Article 10 condition for criminal-offence data; APD required.</td></tr><tr><td>Legal claims, complaints and regulatory cooperation</td><td>Legal obligation / legitimate interests</td><td>Article 9(2)(f) where necessary for legal claims; disclose minimally.</td></tr><tr><td>Direct marketing</td><td>Consent where PECR requires it; otherwise assess legitimate interests</td><td>Never infer or use sexual, health or other special-category chat content for advertising or targeting.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>4.2 LIA A – service security and fraud prevention</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Purpose test</td><td>Protect users, intimate data, accounts, payment access and platform integrity; detect credential abuse, fraud and unauthorised access.</td></tr><tr><td>Necessity test</td><td>Security logging, rate limits, audit events and restricted investigation are less intrusive than unrestricted monitoring and are necessary to operate safely.</td></tr><tr><td>Balancing test</td><td>Users reasonably expect security controls. Data is limited, access restricted, retention short, and findings are not used for unrelated decisions.</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Legitimate interests may be relied upon, subject to the stated controls and the user’s right to object where applicable.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>4.3 LIA B – sampled relationship-quality review</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Purpose test</td><td>Check whether Aimee is maintaining continuity, boundaries and relationship quality; identify missed safety issues and material defects.</td></tr><tr><td>Necessity test</td><td>Aggregate metrics alone cannot reveal contextual failures. A limited human sample is necessary, but blanket reading is not.</td></tr><tr><td>Balancing test</td><td>Intimate content creates high intrusion. Controls: separate optional explicit consent for sensitive QA, senior-only access, pseudonymisation, documented purpose, timed sessions, audit, random sampling and identity suppression.</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Use legitimate interests only for ordinary personal data. Do not rely on legitimate interests alone for special-category content. Safeguarding review follows its own legal conditions and is not dependent on QA consent.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>4.4 LIA C – prioritised safeguarding review</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Purpose test</td><td>Protect people, investigate serious risk and prevent/detect unlawful acts.</td></tr><tr><td>Necessity test</td><td>Prior flags, elevated specialist routing and high imagery volume are used only to select proportionate review candidates, not to determine guilt or risk automatically.</td></tr><tr><td>Balancing test</td><td>Potential intrusion is reduced through pseudonymisation, senior roles, audit, case-specific identity requests and provisional flag language.</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>Legitimate interests may support ordinary-data processing. Apply the APD and relevant Schedule 1 condition to special-category/criminal-offence data.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="appropriate-policy">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Appropriate Policy Document</span><span class="document-description">Schedule 1 safeguards, data-protection principles, retention and accountability.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>5. Appropriate Policy Document (APD)</h2>
<p>This document is adopted for special-category and criminal-offence processing relying on relevant conditions in Schedule 1 to the Data Protection Act 2018, including paragraph 10 (preventing or detecting unlawful acts) and paragraph 18 (safeguarding children and individuals at risk).</p>
<h2>5.1 Compliance with the data-protection principles</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Engram procedure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lawfulness, fairness and transparency</td><td>Record Article 6 and Article 9/10 conditions; explain AI processing and human review; do not describe automated flags as findings; provide complaint and rights routes.</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose limitation</td><td>Use safeguarding data only for safety, investigation, legal compliance and tightly connected learning. Do not use it for marketing or unrelated profiling.</td></tr><tr><td>Data minimisation</td><td>Store category, severity, message reference and necessary excerpt rather than unnecessary copies; pseudonymise routine HQ views; reveal identity only when necessary.</td></tr><tr><td>Accuracy</td><td>Treat flags as provisional; retain the member’s context and Aimee’s response; permit false-positive correction; record reviewer outcome.</td></tr><tr><td>Storage limitation</td><td>Apply the retention schedule, review open cases, delete false positives promptly, and document any legal/safeguarding hold.</td></tr><tr><td>Integrity and confidentiality</td><td>Senior-only roles, timed review sessions, audit logs, least privilege, secure providers, authenticated media access and incident response.</td></tr><tr><td>Accountability</td><td>Maintain DPIA, ROPA, APD, reviewer training, access reviews, case records, complaint handling, breach logs and supplier due diligence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>5.2 Retention and erasure</h2>
<p>Special-category and criminal-offence data is retained only for the period assigned in section 6. False positives are normally erased or de-identified within 12 months. Serious substantiated incidents may be retained for up to six years where necessary for safeguarding continuity, legal claims or regulatory accountability. Each extension must be documented and reviewed at least annually.</p>
<h2>5.3 Responsibilities and review</h2>
<ul>
<li>The Data Protection Lead maintains this APD and the relevant ROPA entries.</li>
<li>The Senior Safeguarding Lead approves case retention, disclosure and closure standards.</li>
<li>The Technical Owner verifies access controls, deletion and audit integrity.</li>
<li>This APD is retained while the processing continues and for at least six months after it ends, in accordance with applicable statutory requirements.</li>
</ul></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="retention">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Retention and deletion schedule</span><span class="document-description">Retention periods, deletion routines, backups, legal holds and evidence of erasure.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>6. Data retention and deletion schedule</h2>
<p>Proposed periods for director approval. Configure systems to enforce them and document exceptions.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Standard retention</th><th>Backup/provider period</th><th>Exception / hold</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Account/profile</td><td>Active account; delete live record within 30 days of verified closure</td><td>Backups expire within 90 days</td><td>Fraud, dispute or legal hold</td></tr><tr><td>Chats, memories and relationship ratings</td><td>Active account; delete within 30 days of closure or verified erasure request</td><td>Backups 90 days</td><td>Open complaint, safeguarding or legal hold; retain only necessary material</td></tr><tr><td>Images and voice notes</td><td>Same as chat; user deletion where supported</td><td>Backups 90 days</td><td>Open safety/legal case</td></tr><tr><td>SMS content</td><td>Same as linked conversation</td><td>Provider delivery logs 12 months</td><td>Dispute or safety case</td></tr><tr><td>Payment/accounting records</td><td>6 years after the end of the relevant financial year</td><td>As required by finance controls</td><td>Tax investigation/legal hold</td></tr><tr><td>Consent records and privacy-notice version</td><td>Life of account plus 6 years</td><td>N/A</td><td>Legal claim</td></tr><tr><td>False-positive safety flags</td><td>12 months after closure</td><td>N/A</td><td>Pattern investigation approved by Safeguarding Lead</td></tr><tr><td>Low/medium resolved safeguarding cases</td><td>24 months after resolution or account closure, whichever is later</td><td>N/A</td><td>Documented safeguarding need</td></tr><tr><td>High/critical substantiated safeguarding or unlawful-act case</td><td>Up to 6 years after closure/resolution</td><td>N/A</td><td>Annual necessity review; longer only with legal advice</td></tr><tr><td>HQ review sessions and reviewer notes</td><td>3 years</td><td>N/A</td><td>Serious case linked record up to 6 years</td></tr><tr><td>Identity reveal requests and access audit</td><td>6 years</td><td>N/A</td><td>None unless litigation hold</td></tr><tr><td>Authentication/security logs</td><td>12 months</td><td>N/A</td><td>Substantiated incident up to 6 years</td></tr><tr><td>Data protection complaints</td><td>3 years after final response</td><td>N/A</td><td>Legal/regulatory case</td></tr><tr><td>Rights requests / SAR records</td><td>3 years after completion</td><td>N/A</td><td>Legal/regulatory case</td></tr><tr><td>Personal data breach log</td><td>6 years after closure</td><td>N/A</td><td>Open regulatory matter</td></tr><tr><td>Age-assurance result/token</td><td>Active account plus 12 months</td><td>No identity document retained by Engram unless strictly necessary</td><td>Regulatory investigation</td></tr><tr><td>Age-assurance vendor identity material</td><td>Vendor-defined minimum period; Engram must verify</td><td>Vendor-controlled</td><td>Must be documented in DPA and notice</td></tr><tr><td>Job/cron temporary audio and processing files</td><td>Delete immediately after successful processing; maximum 24 hours on failure</td><td>N/A</td><td>Technical incident investigation</td></tr><tr><td>Backups</td><td>Rolling maximum 90 days</td><td>Encrypted and access-restricted</td><td>Restore only for disaster recovery; deletion propagates on expiry</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>Deletion controls</h2>
<ul>
<li>Automated monthly report identifying data past retention.</li>
<li>Deletion from live databases, private media directories, indexes and derived memory tables.</li>
<li>Provider deletion requests where a provider retains customer content beyond the API transaction.</li>
<li>Backup expiry rather than destructive editing of immutable backups, with access blocked and deletion propagated after restore.</li>
<li>Written legal/safeguarding hold with owner, scope, reason, review date and release decision.</li>
</ul></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="human-review">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Senior human review and access control</span><span class="document-description">Pseudonymous review, selection criteria, timed sessions, identity reveal and audit requirements.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>7. Senior human-review and access-control policy</h2>
<h2>7.1 Policy statement</h2>
<p>Full chat logs may be reviewed only by approved Senior Safeguarding reviewers or HQ Directors. Review is a controlled exception for safeguarding, complaints, support, legal compliance or proportionate sampled quality assurance. It is not routine monitoring and must never be used for curiosity, entertainment, staff gossip, marketing or personal reasons.</p>
<h2>7.2 Review selection</h2>
<ul>
<li>Random, deterministic monthly spot checks. The initial system rate is 12% and may be reduced following risk review.</li>
<li>Accounts with previous safeguarding flags or unresolved high-severity concerns.</li>
<li>Accounts with erotic-specialist routing above the platform average.</li>
<li>Accounts with user-shared imagery above the platform average.</li>
<li>Specific complaint, support issue, suspected system failure, legal request or documented safety concern.</li>
</ul>
<p>Selection is a prompt for proportionate review, not a finding that the user has behaved unlawfully or unsafely.</p>
<h2>7.3 Required workflow</h2>
<p>1. Reviewer signs in using an individually assigned administrator account with multi-factor authentication where available.</p>
<p>2. Reviewer starts a time-limited member review session, selects an approved purpose and writes a specific reason.</p>
<p>3. Member identity remains pseudonymous. The reviewer samples only the amount necessary to answer the review question.</p>
<p>4. If direct identity is genuinely required, the reviewer submits a second request explaining why pseudonymous review is insufficient.</p>
<p>5. Identity is displayed only to that reviewer for a short period and every reveal is audited.</p>
<p>6. Reviewer records outcome, actions, false positives and any escalation. No offline copy is made unless approved for a specific case.</p>
<h2>7.4 Prohibited actions</h2>
<ul>
<li>Downloading or copying complete transcripts “just in case”.</li>
<li>Searching for known people, colleagues, celebrities or acquaintances without a documented case purpose.</li>
<li>Using personal devices, screenshots or messaging apps to share review content.</li>
<li>Revealing identity to another person who has not independently been authorised for the case.</li>
<li>Using erotic content or imagery for training, demonstrations or humour.</li>
<li>Treating Aimee’s ratings, erotic routing or safety flags as proof of character, intent or criminal conduct.</li>
</ul>
<h2>7.5 Monitoring and enforcement</h2>
<p>HQ Directors review reviewer access quarterly and audit a sample of sessions monthly. Suspicious access, repeated identity requests or access outside work duties is investigated as a security incident and may result in immediate suspension, disciplinary action and breach assessment.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="safeguarding-policy">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Safeguarding and escalation policy</span><span class="document-description">Triage, categories, serious-risk decisions, disclosures, case closure and learning.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>8. Safeguarding and escalation policy</h2>
<h2>8.1 Scope and limitations</h2>
<p>Aimee is an AI companion, not an emergency, medical, mental-health, legal or law-enforcement service. Automated detection can miss context and can be wrong. Safeguarding classifications support human judgement and proportionate intervention; they are not diagnoses or factual determinations.</p>
<h2>8.2 Safeguarding categories</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Examples</th><th>Default priority</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adult erotic content</td><td>Consensual adult intimacy routed to specialist model</td><td>Information / QA, not inherently a safeguarding concern</td></tr><tr><td>Coercion / non-consent</td><td>Threats, pressure, intoxication, incapacity, forced acts</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Minor / age uncertainty</td><td>User or subject may be under 18; ambiguous age in sexual context</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual content involving minors</td><td>Sexualisation, grooming, exploitation or imagery involving a child</td><td>Critical</td></tr><tr><td>Grooming / exploitation</td><td>Manipulation, exchange, trafficking or abuse indicators</td><td>Critical</td></tr><tr><td>Self-harm / suicide</td><td>Ideation, plan, means or imminent intent</td><td>Medium to Critical based on immediacy</td></tr><tr><td>Serious violence / threats</td><td>Credible threat, plan, target, means or imminent harm</td><td>High to Critical</td></tr><tr><td>Potential unlawful acts</td><td>Serious planned or admitted unlawful conduct</td><td>Case-dependent</td></tr><tr><td>Stalking / harassment / doxxing</td><td>Targeted surveillance, threats or personal-data exposure</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Trafficking / forced exploitation</td><td>Control, movement, forced sexual/labour exploitation</td><td>Critical</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>8.3 Triage</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Critical</td><td>Immediate senior review; preserve minimum necessary evidence; consider emergency services or specialist authority; Director/Safeguarding Lead decision recorded.</td></tr><tr><td>High</td><td>Senior review as soon as practicable, normally same day; assess credibility, context, identity need and external escalation.</td></tr><tr><td>Medium</td><td>Review within two working days; provide safe in-product response and monitor recurrence where justified.</td></tr><tr><td>Low / information</td><td>No identity reveal. Use aggregate or sampled QA unless another concern emerges.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>8.4 External escalation principles</h2>
<ul>
<li>Call 999/112 where there is a reasonable belief of immediate danger to life or serious injury and sufficient actionable information is available.</li>
<li>Use 101 or the appropriate specialist/local safeguarding route for non-immediate concerns where disclosure is lawful and necessary.</li>
<li>For child sexual exploitation or abuse concerns, obtain senior/legal guidance and use the appropriate UK police, CEOP/NCA or other recognised reporting channel. Do not conduct an amateur investigation or unnecessarily reproduce illegal material.</li>
<li>Disclose only the minimum information necessary, use a secure channel and record the legal basis, recipient, decision-maker, information disclosed and outcome.</li>
<li>Do not promise confidentiality where a serious safeguarding or legal disclosure may be necessary.</li>
</ul>
<h2>8.5 Self-harm and emotional crisis</h2>
<p>Aimee should respond compassionately, encourage real-world human help and provide appropriate crisis signposting. Human review or external disclosure is considered only where the threshold, available information and legal basis justify it. The service must not imply that it continuously monitors users or can guarantee emergency intervention.</p>
<h2>8.6 Quality and learning</h2>
<p>The Safeguarding Lead reviews critical cases, false negatives, false positives, model refusals and missed route decisions at least quarterly. Improvements must not involve retaining more intimate data than necessary.</p></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="rights-procedure">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Data rights and complaints procedure</span><span class="document-description">Internal workflow for requests, identity checks, complaints, outcomes and records.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>9. Data subject rights and complaints procedure</h2>
<h2>9.1 Public channels</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Privacy email</td><td>[TO COMPLETE: dedicated privacy email address]</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic complaint / rights form</td><td>The public Privacy, Policy &amp; Safeguarding page supplied with this pack</td></tr><tr><td>Postal address</td><td>34–40 Witham, Witham, Hull, England, HU9 1BY</td></tr><tr><td>ICO registration</td><td>[TO COMPLETE: ICO registration number, if registered]</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>9.2 Complaint requirements</h2>
<p>From 19 June 2026, the organisation must operate a data-protection complaints process. Engram will provide an electronic route, acknowledge a complaint within 30 days and respond without undue delay. A complaint register must record receipt, acknowledgement, investigation, communications, outcome, remedial action and closure.</p>
<h2>9.3 Rights workflow</h2>
<p>1. Log the request or complaint and assign a reference.</p>
<p>2. Acknowledge promptly. For formal data-protection complaints, in all cases no later than 30 days.</p>
<p>3. Confirm scope and verify identity proportionately. Do not routinely request copies of identity documents where account authentication or lower-risk checks are sufficient.</p>
<p>4. Locate data across profile, messages, media, voice, SMS, memory, ratings, HQ records, billing references and provider systems where applicable.</p>
<p>5. Apply third-party rights, safeguarding and legal exemptions carefully and document any redaction or refusal.</p>
<p>6. Respond normally within one month for UK GDPR rights requests, subject to lawful extensions. Explain decisions in plain language.</p>
<p>7. Provide an internal escalation route and explain the right to complain to the ICO and seek a judicial remedy.</p>
<h2>9.4 Rights covered</h2>
<ul>
<li>Access to personal information and meaningful information about relevant AI/profiling logic.</li>
<li>Correction of inaccurate profile or account data and a route to challenge misleading relationship/safety records.</li>
<li>Erasure where applicable, including linked media and derived memories.</li>
<li>Restriction and objection, including objection to legitimate-interests processing.</li>
<li>Data portability for information provided by the user where the legal conditions apply.</li>
<li>Withdrawal of explicit consent without affecting earlier lawful processing.</li>
<li>Human review where a solely automated significant decision is made. Aimee must not use relationship ratings for legal or similarly significant decisions.</li>
</ul></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="breach-response">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Personal data breach response plan</span><span class="document-description">Containment, risk assessment, notification, communication and post-incident review.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>10. Personal data breach response plan</h2>
<div class="callout warning">72-hour clock<br>Start a breach log as soon as the organisation becomes aware of a suspected personal data breach. If the breach is reportable, notify the ICO without undue delay and, where feasible, within 72 hours. Document the decision even where no report is made.</div>
<h2>10.1 Response sequence</h2>
<p>1. Contain: revoke sessions/keys, isolate systems, disable affected endpoint, preserve logs and prevent further disclosure.</p>
<p>2. Escalate internally to the Data Protection Lead, Technical Owner, Director and Safeguarding Lead where intimate or safety data is involved.</p>
<p>3. Establish facts: what happened, dates, systems, people, data categories, volume, encryption, recipients and likely consequences.</p>
<p>4. Assess risk to individuals, taking particular account of intimate chats, identity linkage, minors, health/sexual content, voice/images and blackmail risk.</p>
<p>5. Decide and record whether ICO notification is required. Submit an initial report within 72 hours where required, with follow-up information later if necessary.</p>
<p>6. Notify affected individuals without undue delay where the breach is likely to result in a high risk, using clear protective advice.</p>
<p>7. Remediate, monitor misuse, reset credentials, obtain processor reports and complete a lessons-learned review.</p>
<h2>10.2 Breach log fields</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Record</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reference / discovered</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Incident owner</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Facts and timeline</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>People and data affected</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Risk assessment</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Containment</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>ICO decision and time</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Individual notification</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Root cause / remediation</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr><tr><td>Closure and review</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="processors">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Processor and international-transfer register</span><span class="document-description">Supplier due diligence, contracts, processing locations, safeguards and review status.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>11. Processor and international-transfer register</h2>
<p>Complete the contractual and transfer columns from the signed vendor terms. Do not infer safeguards from a marketing page.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Provider</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Data</th><th>Location</th><th>Contract</th><th>Transfer mechanism</th><th>Due diligence</th><th>Priority</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>WordPress/hosting provider</td><td>Hosting, database, media, backups</td><td>All service data</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>Article 28 DPA</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>CRITICAL</td></tr><tr><td>Anthropic</td><td>Primary conversational AI, classification/analysis</td><td>Relevant prompts, context, images where used</td><td>[TO COMPLETE from DPA]</td><td>DPA / API terms</td><td>Adequacy or IDTA/Addendum + TRA if restricted</td><td>Confirm retention and training controls</td><td>CRITICAL</td></tr><tr><td>OpenRouter and selected model provider</td><td>Adult intimacy specialist routing</td><td>Eligible prompt/context and response</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>DPA / terms plus downstream provider map</td><td>IDTA/Addendum + TRA as required</td><td>Identify every downstream model provider</td><td>CRITICAL</td></tr><tr><td>Deepgram</td><td>Voice transcription</td><td>Voice audio and metadata</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>DPA</td><td>Transfer safeguard as required</td><td>Verify deletion/retention</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>ElevenLabs</td><td>Text-to-speech</td><td>Aimee reply text, voice ID, generated audio metadata</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>DPA</td><td>Transfer safeguard as required</td><td>Verify voice/data use terms</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Stripe</td><td>Subscription and payment processing</td><td>Contact, customer/payment IDs, plan and transaction data</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>DPA / controller terms as applicable</td><td>Vendor safeguards</td><td>Do not store card data locally</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>GoCardless</td><td>UK recurring Pay by Bank authorisation and payment processing</td><td>Customer, Billing Request, mandate/payment IDs, authorised plan and transaction data</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>DPA / controller terms as applicable</td><td>Vendor safeguards</td><td>Verify creditor identity and do not store bank credentials locally</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>FireText</td><td>SMS delivery</td><td>Phone number, SMS content and delivery metadata</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>DPA</td><td>Confirm UK/EEA routing and subprocessors</td><td>PECR and security review</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Brave Search</td><td>Optional live information retrieval</td><td>Search query stripped of unnecessary identity</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>Terms/DPA as applicable</td><td>Confirm</td><td>Do not send full transcript</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>OpenWeather</td><td>Weather context</td><td>Location query, normally city/region rather than precise address</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>Terms/DPA as applicable</td><td>Confirm</td><td>Minimise location granularity</td><td>Medium</td></tr><tr><td>Email provider</td><td>Privacy forms, complaints and support</td><td>Contact and correspondence</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>Article 28 DPA</td><td>Confirm</td><td>Dedicated restricted mailbox</td><td>High</td></tr><tr><td>Age-assurance provider</td><td>Highly effective adult verification</td><td>Verification data and pass/fail token</td><td>[TO COMPLETE]</td><td>Article 28 DPA or controller allocation</td><td>Confirm and complete TRA</td><td>Minimise; Engram stores token only</td><td>CRITICAL</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p><strong>Checkout cutover note:</strong> GoCardless is the sole provider for every currently available new checkout. Stripe remains in this register only because existing card subscriptions, old pending checkout sessions, completed purchases and statutory financial records still require controlled runoff, reconciliation or retention.</p>
<h2>Supplier checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>Signed Article 28 terms where the provider is a processor, including confidentiality, security, sub-processors, breach notice, deletion/return, assistance and audit information.</li>
<li>Documented controller/processor role allocation where a provider acts independently for some purposes.</li>
<li>Current sub-processor list, processing locations and prior notice of material changes.</li>
<li>UK adequacy decision or an executed UK IDTA / UK Addendum plus documented transfer risk assessment for restricted transfers.</li>
<li>Provider retention, abuse monitoring, model-training and opt-out settings verified and recorded.</li>
<li>Security assurance, incident contacts and deletion testing.</li>
</ul></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="age-assurance">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Age assurance and adult-content compliance record</span><span class="document-description">Deployment gates, evidence, testing and governance for adults-only functionality.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>12. Age assurance and adult-content compliance record</h2>
<div class="callout warning">Critical legal gap<br>A self-declared age field is not highly effective age assurance. Because Aimee can route users to erotic/adult output, UK access to that output must remain disabled until a suitable age-assurance method is integrated, tested and recorded. This is an Online Safety Act / Ofcom issue in addition to data protection.</div>
<h2>12.1 Service assessment</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Current draft assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Does the service enable user-to-user sharing?</td><td>No, based on the current product design.</td></tr><tr><td>Does it search multiple websites/databases?</td><td>Some live-information functions may search external sources; legal scope must be reviewed against actual user-facing functionality.</td></tr><tr><td>Can the provider generate pornographic/adult content?</td><td>Yes, an erotic/intimacy specialist route exists.</td></tr><tr><td>Part 5 age-assurance duty potentially applicable?</td><td>Yes. Obtain specialist Online Safety Act advice and treat highly effective age assurance as mandatory before adult output.</td></tr><tr><td>Current age control</td><td>Self-declared age only in the reviewed code. Not sufficient for adult-content access.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>12.2 Implementation record</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Required evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Provider and method</td><td>[TO COMPLETE: vendor, method(s), contract and launch date]</td></tr><tr><td>Highly effective assessment</td><td>Evidence of technical accuracy, independence, robustness and effectiveness at preventing child access.</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy design</td><td>Data minimisation, tokenised result, no unnecessary identity document held by Engram, retention and rights route.</td></tr><tr><td>Access control</td><td>Adult route blocked before successful check; re-check triggers; no bypass through API/SMS/voice.</td></tr><tr><td>Circumvention controls</td><td>Testing for VPN/device/account sharing and publicly available bypasses; proportional monitoring.</td></tr><tr><td>Failure and appeal</td><td>Safe failure, user support and a route to challenge an incorrect result.</td></tr><tr><td>Record keeping</td><td>Written record of method, implementation, testing, incidents, vendor changes and periodic review.</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="consent">
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Consent and just-in-time transparency standard</span><span class="document-description">Separate consent records, withdrawal, onboarding and contextual notices.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
<div class="document-body"><h2>13. Consent and just-in-time transparency standard</h2>
<h2>13.1 Onboarding notices</h2>
<p>Do not use one bundled “I agree” checkbox for every purpose. Contract acceptance, explicit consent, marketing consent and age assurance must be separate.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>Draft wording</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Privacy acknowledgement (not consent)</td><td>“I have read the Privacy, Human Review &amp; Safeguarding Notice and understand how Aimee uses my information.”</td></tr><tr><td>Core special-category explicit consent</td><td>“I explicitly consent to Ampera EV Ltd, through Engram Intelligence, and its contracted AI service providers processing sensitive information I choose to share with Aimee, including information about my health, sex life or sexual orientation, where this is necessary to respond to me, maintain our conversation and apply safety measures. I understand I can withdraw this consent, but Aimee may then be unable to process conversations containing that information.”</td></tr><tr><td>Optional human quality-review explicit consent</td><td>“I explicitly consent to a limited, pseudonymised sample of my conversations being reviewed by authorised senior safeguarding or management staff to assess Aimee’s relationship quality and identify missed safety issues. This is optional. I can withdraw it at any time. Safeguarding, complaint and legal reviews may still take place where lawful and necessary.”</td></tr><tr><td>Marketing consent</td><td>“I would like to receive Aimee and Engram Intelligence news and offers by [email/SMS]. I can unsubscribe at any time.”</td></tr><tr><td>Adult feature notice</td><td>“Adult features are for verified adults only. I understand that age assurance is required and that adult access will be unavailable if verification is not completed.”</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>13.2 Just-in-time notices</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table class="policy-table"><thead><tr><th>Moment</th><th>Notice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Before uploading an image</td><td>“Images may be stored with your chat and processed by our contracted AI provider so Aimee can respond. Do not upload someone else’s private image without permission.”</td></tr><tr><td>Before first voice note</td><td>“Your voice note is sent to our transcription provider, stored privately with your chat and used to create Aimee’s reply. It is not used to identify you by voice.”</td></tr><tr><td>Before enabling SMS</td><td>“Your phone number and message content will be processed by our SMS provider. Service messages are separate from marketing consent.”</td></tr><tr><td>Before optional QA consent</td><td>“This does not mean staff continuously read chats. Reviews are sampled, pseudonymised, reasoned, time-limited and audited.”</td></tr><tr><td>Before identity reveal to staff</td><td>No user-facing prompt is required at the moment of a justified safeguarding review, but the privacy notice must explain the process and access must be audited.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2>13.3 Consent records</h2>
<ul>
<li>User ID, consent purpose, exact wording/version, affirmative action, timestamp and source interface.</li>
<li>Withdrawal timestamp and effect on features.</li>
<li>No pre-ticked boxes, silence or bundled acceptance.</li>
<li>Re-consent after a material purpose change.</li>
<li>Retain proof under the retention schedule without retaining unnecessary conversation content.</li>
</ul></div>
</details>
<details class="policy-document" id="action-plan" open>
<summary class="document-summary"><span><span class="document-kicker"><span>Controlled internal document</span><span>•</span><span><?php echo esc_html($document_status); ?></span></span><span class="document-title">Implementation action plan</span><span class="document-description">Outstanding actions, owners, priorities and evidence required before adoption.</span></span><span class="chevron" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
